Nigeria’s higher education sector has been undergoing a significant shift as the number of private universities continues to grow rapidly.
The country’s National Universities Commission (NUC) recently licensed 11 new private universities, bringing the total to 159. This is part of a broader expansion following President Bola Tinubu’s earlier approval of 67 new tertiary institutions nationwide. These include universities, polytechnics, colleges of education and monotechnics – a decision aimed at tackling the longstanding issue of limited access to university education.
The 11 newly licensed universities are:
•	New City University, Aiyetoro, Ogun State;
•	Lens University, Ilemona, Kwara State;
•	Kevin Eze University, Mgbowo, Enugu State;
•	Southern Atlantic University, Uyo, Akwa Ibom State;
•	University of Fortune, Igbotako, Ondo State;
•	Minaret University, Ikirun, Osun State;
•	Abdulrasaq Abubakar Toyin University, Ganmo, Kwara State;
•	Monarch University, Iyesi Ota, Ogun State;
•	Tonnie Iredia, University of Communication, Benin, Edo State;
•	Isaac Balami University of Aeronautics and Management, Lagos State; and
•	Eranova University, Kuje, Federal Capital Territory.
The expansion has been hailed by many as a step in the right direction, including Professor Ishaq Oloyede, the registrar of the body that oversees higher education admission in Nigeria, the Joint Admissions and Matriculation Board, or JAMB. “It demonstrates Tinubu’s commitment to expanding access to tertiary education,” Oloyede said . However, it has also sparked concern among education stakeholders who warn that quantity must not come at the expense of quality. Chief Afe Babalola, the senior advocate of Nigeria and founder of Afe Babalola University, Ado Ekiti, has warned the government to halt the mass approval and prioritise quality education over quantity. Commendable drive Speaking to University World News, Professor Mahfouz Adedimeji, pioneer vice-chancellor of Ahman Pategi University and vice-chancellor of the African School of Economics (The Pan-African University of Excellence) in Abuja, said the drive to increase access is commendable. He pointed out that the United States and Canada have thousands of universities, while China’s number of top-ranked universities compares to Nigeria’s total number – but the increase must be approached cautiously. “Expansion, in itself, isn’t a problem. It’s access that we’re trying to solve, and private universities help bridge that gap. Victor Hugo said that whoever opens a school door closes a prison. We should rather expand schools and universities than allow criminals and criminality to expand.” Adedimeji emphasised the need for long-term commitment from university heads. “Running a university is capital-intensive. It’s not a sprint; it’s a marathon. Just like you nurture a child for years before they can stand on their own, a university needs sustained funding and care, especially in the first 10 to 20 years. Expansion and numbers should never compromise standards,” Adedimeji explained and warned that, without proper funding and planning, many new private institutions risk becoming glorified secondary schools. “If you give birth to a child and leave them to fend for themselves, they become vulnerable. Universities, especially private ones, must be nurtured with vision and resources.” The need for expansion Dr Praise Ejigboye, a private higher education analyst and lecturer from Landmark University, agrees that the rapid rise in private universities is essential, especially given Nigeria’s massive youth population and growing demand for university slots. “Statistically, public universities alone cannot absorb the number of students seeking admission each year,” he told University World News, adding that private universities provide a valuable alternative, but he won’t support compromising quality just to meet demand. Ejigboye praised the regulatory roles of the NUC and other bodies like the Council for the Regulation of Engineering in Nigeria, or COREN, saying they have systems in place to ensure only qualified institutions receive licences. “The accreditation process acts as a check and balance mechanism. Institutions that fail to meet the criteria are denied approval, and even after licensing, there should be regular quality assurance checks,” he said. He pointed to the stellar performance of graduates from private universities like Covenant and Landmark, saying they consistently hold their own against graduates from public universities, especially in postgraduate programmes and the workplace. “What we need is consistency. Institutions must uphold the ethics and standards already laid down. And the regulators must keep updating those standards in line with changing societal needs.” Quality Dr Tunji Alausa, the Nigerian minister of education, echoed concerns about the quality of education: “We must acknowledge an uncomfortable truth that, while we now have 159 licensed private universities, too many are failing to meet the quality standards Nigeria demands,” he said. Adedimeji also stressed the importance of integrating practical skills and entrepreneurship into university education, and not just private universities. “At the African School of Economics, we design programmes that turn students into job creators, not just job seekers,” he said. “We explore the economic potential of each discipline and equip students with real-world tools.” Ejigboye also advocates involving industry professionals in teaching, particularly in areas like business and technology. “Learning must be tied to practice. Students should not just memorise theories; they must engage with the realities of the workplace,” he added. New universities and their aims Dr Eze Everest, the director of academic planning at Kevin Eze University, Mgbowo in Enugu State, one of the newly licensed institutions, told University World News that its mission is to provide students with knowledge, confidence, and character in a supportive environment. The university aims to equip students with practical skills, going beyond the minimum academic standards set by Nigeria’s educational authorities. “We introduce digital technology, including cybersecurity and information technology,” Everest explained. “One of our priorities is digital technology and entrepreneurship that even meets the standards in UK universities.” The university plans a BSc in statistics and data science that will enable students to acquire skills, become independent and progress beyond mere theory. It is also working towards introducing environmental science programmes and digital literacy, and has partnered with experienced academics to provide high-quality education and training. Similarly, Pastor James Bassey, the founder of the Southern Atlantic University in Akwa Ibom State, said the institution would focus on agriculture and also fostering entrepreneurship, job creation, community development, and other practical skills which are the tools needed to succeed in an ever-changing world. Positive and negative consequences Professor Yusuf Noah, the vice-chairman of the Committee of Vice Chancellors and Registrars of Private Universities in Nigeria as well as the vice-chancellor of Al-Hikmah University, told University World News the recent surge in private university approvals in Nigeria can have both positive and negative consequences. “If well-managed, this development can increase access to higher education for millions of Nigerian youths who are yet to benefit from it and struggle to access education. “However, if not properly regulated, it could lead to a decline in educational standards and quality,” he said. He further commented that, to ensure that quality standards are maintained in these new institutions, regulatory bodies like the NUC must play a vital role, adding that the current accreditation exercise conducted by NUC is a commendable effort in this direction. “Additionally, other regulatory bodies such as the Federal Ministry of Education, Joint Admissions and Matriculation Board, and Tertiary Education Trust Fund should also commence regular monitoring activities to complement NUC’s efforts. “By working together, these bodies can help reposition Nigerian universities for global excellence and ensure that students receive high-quality education that prepares them for the challenges of the future.” He advised the newly licensed universities to maintain standards and added that the Committee of Vice-Chancellors and Registrars of Private Universities in Nigeria, as a powerful pressure group, has consistently submitted proposals to the National Assembly as well sensitising stakeholders on many issues on higher education in Nigeria, including the proliferation of universities in the country.
